        content: `**Devon** is **34**, a senior engineer at a tech company earning **$280k** with a strong 401(k) plan that allows **after-tax contributions** and **in-plan Roth conversions**. He's above the Roth IRA phaseout ($165k single), so direct Roth is closed.\n\nHere's his 2026 stack:\n\n- **Roth 401(k) elective**: $23,500 (he chooses Roth over Traditional given his tax outlook)\n- **Employer match**: $14,000 (5% match) — this goes pre-tax into Traditional 401(k)\n- **After-tax 401(k) → Roth conversion (Mega Backdoor)**: $32,500 (fills the $70k overall cap)\n- **Backdoor Roth IRA**: $7,000 (Traditional IRA → converted same week)\n- **HSA**: $4,400 (HDHP, triple tax-free)\n\n**Total going into tax-free accounts: $66,900**, plus $14k pre-tax match. After **20 years at 8%**, just the Roth stack compounds to roughly **$3.1M** — every penny **withdrawal-tax-free**. That's the playbook high-income professionals use.`,
