        content: `**This week:**\n\n1. **Check your income vs phaseouts.** Single filer making **$120k** with a 401(k)? Your Traditional IRA deduction is **fully phased out** (above $89k cap) — but you can do a **direct Roth IRA contribution** (under $150k). Run your number against the chart.\n\n2. **If above the Roth direct-contribution cap**, execute a **Backdoor Roth**. But FIRST: check if you have any pre-tax IRA balances (rollovers, old SEP, SIMPLE). If yes, **roll them into your 401(k)** before doing the conversion — most plans accept this within 4-6 weeks.\n\n3. **Ask HR these exact questions** for the Mega Backdoor:\n   - Does our 401(k) plan allow **after-tax (non-Roth) employee contributions**?\n   - Does it support **in-service Roth conversions** (or in-plan Roth rollovers)?\n   - Both yes? You've got the Mega Backdoor.\n\n4. **Set up annual automation.** Schedule January contributions to max out your Roth IRA via Backdoor by **April 15** the latest. Compound growth doesn't reward procrastinators.`,
