        type: "example",
        title: "Two Investors, March 2020",
        content: `**Anna and Jake** both had **$300,000** in **VTI** on **February 19, 2020** — the pre-COVID market peak.

By **March 23, 2020**, the S&P 500 had crashed **-34%**. Both portfolios were worth **$198,000**. Anna stared at her screen. The news said **'WORST RECESSION SINCE 1929.'** Her **loss aversion** kicked in. She sold everything and moved to a **HYSA at 1.5%**.

**Jake had set up automatic $1,000/month contributions** in 2019 and never logged in. He didn't even know he had crashed. His automation kept buying **VTI** at the lows: $128, $130, $135.

**By December 31, 2020**: VTI had rallied **+75%** off the lows. Jake's portfolio: **$362,000**. Anna's HYSA: **$200,200** (she earned $2,200 interest).

**The five-year follow-up (Dec 2024)**: VTI returned roughly **+95% cumulative** from March 2020. Jake: **~$580,000**. Anna, scared to re-enter, sat in cash: **~$220,000**. Cost of one panic sale: **~$360,000**.`,
